>
  <p>
    A code arrives on your phone while you are signing in on your laptop. A
    password form asks you to enter the same new password twice. Your connection
    drops just when you need a saved login. These are the moments we have been
    working on since our <a href="/blog/email-codes-and-easier-sign-ins">September 16 update</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This roundup covers changes through October 6: extension releases
    <strong>1.2.14–1.2.16</strong>, <strong>Authier for Android 0.1.6</strong>,
    and two follow-up improvements coming in the next builds. Alongside SMS
    codes and inline filling, you can now choose how to recover a lost master
    device and receive device sign-in notifications on Android.
  </p>

  <h2>SMS codes from your phone, in your browser</h2>
  <p>
    The extension now brings text-message verification codes into the same popup
    as your Gmail codes. Email appears under <strong>From your email</strong>;
    SMS appears under <strong>From your phone</strong>. A red dot on the toolbar
    tells you when a new code is available, including when the popup is closed.
    Open your unlocked vault, then click a masked code to copy and reveal it.
  </p>
  <p>There are two ways to bring SMS codes into Authier:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Authier for Android:</strong> in Settings, enable
      <strong>Send SMS codes while unlocked</strong> and grant Android’s SMS
      permission. Incoming texts are checked on the phone. Authier encrypts the
      extracted code and sender with your vault key before sending them to your
      browser; the message body stays on the phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Messages for Web:</strong> pair it with your phone and keep
      it open in a browser tab. Authier reads codes from rendered unread
      conversation previews and the newest received message in the open
      conversation. It skips earlier messages in the thread.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/blog/october-2026/sms-verification-codes.png"
      alt="A synthetic Google Messages conversation beside the Authier popup, with masked SMS codes from the web tab and an Android phone"
      width="1040"
      height="760"
      loading="eager"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Both SMS sources share one list. This preview shows the 1.2.16 UI with
      synthetic messages and sample codes.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Phone relay needs Authier signed in to the same vault on both devices. A
    locked phone vault sends nothing, and the browser needs an unlocked vault to
    decrypt relayed codes. Choose a timed unlock on Android if you want relay to
    work while Authier is in the background; locking as soon as you leave the
    app stops it. Background checks can be delayed by browser sleep, so delivery
    is not guaranteed to be instant.
  </p>
  <p>
    SMS detection handles four-to-eight-character codes, including common
    localized code labels, while filtering amounts, card numbers, and other
    unrelated numbers. Temporary codes expire from Authier after ten minutes;
    the sender may expire them sooner. They stay separate from your saved vault
    items. A phone-relayed code identifies its sending device, while a web code’s
    source icon opens its Gmail or Messages tab without copying it.
  </p>

  <h2>Fill verification codes where you need them</h2>
  <p>
    On supported HTTPS pages, the Authier logo now appears beside a detected
    verification field or a group of digit boxes. Click it, choose a masked
    suggestion, and Authier fills the code directly into the form. This works
    with Gmail, Google Messages, and Android-relayed codes, including forms
    that appear after the page first loads.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/blog/october-2026/inline-verification-codes.png"
      alt="Authier’s inline picker offering a masked email code beside six verification inputs on a synthetic Shopify form"
      width="1100"
      height="820"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Choose the code beside the form. One selection fills all six boxes in this
      synthetic verification page.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Your vault must be unlocked and autofill enabled. Email suggestions match
    the sender’s domain to the website, while SMS codes remain explicit choices:
    a phone number or sender name cannot reliably identify a website. Only codes
    that fit the field’s length and numeric requirements are offered. Selecting
    one replaces partially entered digits too; you still complete the website’s
    sign-in flow yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose your plan for a lost master device</h2>
  <p>
    New accounts now choose a recovery policy during signup in the extension,
    web vault, and Android app. The default requires
    <strong>one approval from another device and a 48-hour wait</strong>. You
    can choose zero to ten device approvals and a waiting period from five
    minutes to 90 days, plus additional email addresses for recovery updates.
  </p>
  <figure class="recovery-shot">
    <img
      src="/blog/october-2026/master-device-recovery.png"
      alt="Authier’s recovery setup showing one required device approval, a 48-hour waiting period, and optional notification email addresses"
      width="900"
      height="1040"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Set the approval count and waiting period before you need recovery.
      Screenshot addresses are synthetic.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    If you lose your master device, request a reset from the login approval
    screen. Confirm the link sent to your account email within 24 hours; that
    confirmation starts the waiting period. Eligible devices you already had
    can approve through Devices, and signed-in devices can cancel an unexpected
    request. The progress display shows the approval count and when the wait
    ends. Both conditions must be met before the lost master is removed.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need recovery without another available device, choose zero approvals
    in advance. <strong>Your vault password is still required.</strong> This
    recovers device access, not a forgotten password. Extra notification addresses
    receive updates; only your account email receives the confirmation link.
  </p>
  <p>
    The master device can change the policy in the extension’s settings or the
    web vault’s Security page. Pending requests keep the rules they started
    with. Existing accounts retain their email-based recovery policy rather than
    silently gaining a new approval requirement. Our
    <a href="/guides/trusted-device-approval">trusted-device guide</a> explains
    how device access fits into Authier’s security model.
  </p>

  <h2>Android notifications for device sign-ins</h2>
  <p>
    Authier for Android now receives push notifications when another device
    signs in or requests approval, according to your device access policy. Tap
    the notification to open Devices and review the request. If the vault is
    locked, unlock it first; the app then takes you to the device list.
  </p>
  <p>
    Android 13 and newer ask for notification permission. You can revisit it
    through <strong>Settings → Sign-in notifications → Notification settings</strong>.
    Notification delivery depends on the phone’s notification settings and push
    service. The extension’s device-policy chooser also now handles selection,
    saving errors, keyboard focus, and scrolling on small screens more clearly.
  </p>

  <h2>Authenticator labels that identify the account</h2>
  <p>
    Scanning a TOTP QR code in the extension now saves an account-first label
    such as <strong>alex@example.com | Microsoft</strong>. That makes work and
    personal codes easier to distinguish when they belong to the same provider.
    The account email comes from the QR code when available, then the page or a
    recently entered email in the current browser session. If none is available,
    Authier asks you to enter it before saving.
  </p>
  <p>
    When that extra step is needed, the form separates account email and provider
    and previews the saved label. Regular and private browsing keep their session email
    fallbacks separate. These labels apply to newly saved codes; existing
    authenticator entries keep their names.
  </p>

  <h2>Fewer interruptions from forms and connections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Generated passwords fill confirmation fields.</strong> On
      supported signup and password-change forms, choosing a generated password
      also fills its repeat field with the same value. Current-password fields
      stay unchanged, and detection reaches more forms built with web components.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Autofill respects the account you selected.</strong> A username
      already entered on the page takes priority, including Google’s separate
      password step. Ambiguous password-only forms offer an account picker
      instead of guessing. Android Autofill also tolerates missing field hints
      that could previously interrupt filling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Saved items remain available through connection failures.</strong>
      The extension shows locally cached items while checking the server, keeps
      them through failed session renewal, and displays an offline notice when
      it cannot connect. You can search, reveal passwords, and switch views;
      retry sync when the connection returns. Adding items still needs a
      connection when the service cannot be reached.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Connecting a browser populates the vault more reliably.</strong>
      The initial sync continues in the background when the popup closes, and
      received items appear in an already open popup without a manual refresh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Large vaults use the available space.</strong> Card and table
      views fill tall windows, recover after a search finds no matches, and
      resize correctly in Firefox as well as Chromium.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Coming in the next builds</h2>
  <div class="article-callout">
    <p>
      Two more improvements landed after the October 4 release tags and will
      arrive in subsequent builds:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Dismiss a section’s temporary codes together.</strong> Each
        email or SMS section shows its count and a <strong>Dismiss all</strong>
        action. It clears the displayed codes while preserving a code that
        arrived just as you clicked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>See whether Android Autofill is set up.</strong> Settings shows
        whether Authier is the selected provider and offers the appropriate
        setup or settings button. The status refreshes when you return from
        Android settings, including after canceling or switching providers.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Get the updates</h2>
  <p>
    The latest published GitHub releases are
    <a href="https://github.com/authier-pm/authier/releases/tag/v1.2.16-extension">v1.2.16-extension</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/authier-pm/authier/releases/tag/v0.1.6-android">v0.1.6-android</a>.
    Browser store rollout can follow release publication; use the
    <a href="/download#browsers">browser download page</a> for Chrome, Firefox,
    and Edge. Reload existing Gmail and Google Messages tabs after updating so
    the new detection scripts can run.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Android, <a href="/download#android">update through Obtainium or the signed APK</a>.
    Update your existing installation in place to keep the local vault. For the
    complete release window, browse the
    <a href="https://github.com/authier-pm/authier/compare/v1.2.13-extension...v1.2.16-extension">extension changes</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/authier-pm/authier/compare/v0.1.5-android...v0.1.6-android">Android changes</a>.
  </p>
